{
    "title": "Analysis of Residential Self-Selection Effects on Household Travel Behavior",
    "authors": [
        {
            "name": "Bryan K. A. Bryan",
            "gender": "Male"
        }
    ],
    "abstract": "This paper analyzes the effects of residential self-selection on household travel behavior using a sample of 2,511 households from the National Household Travel Survey (NHTS). The results indicate that households tend to select their residences based on factors that influence travel behavior, such as proximity to employment, education, and healthcare facilities. The study also examines the relationships between land-use characteristics and travel patterns, finding that increased density and connectivity are associated with lower levels of personal vehicle use.",
